  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "Soviet engineers drilled twelve kilometres down, then stopped. The hole is still there, welded shut, in the Arctic.",
        "visual_query": "arctic russian tundra industrial site",
        "visual_fallback": "arctic tundra"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Work started on the Kola Peninsula on the twenty-fourth of May, 1970. The target was simply to see how deep a hole could go.",
        "visual_query": "soviet drilling rig derrick snow",
        "visual_fallback": "drilling rig"
      },
      {
        "narration": "By 1989 the deepest branch reached 12,262 metres. The borehole itself is barely wider than a dinner plate.",
        "visual_query": "narrow borehole steel casing pipe",
        "visual_fallback": "steel casing"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Five years earlier they had already lost five kilometres of drill string in the hole. It twisted off and stayed there.",
        "visual_query": "broken drill pipe rusted machinery",
        "visual_fallback": "drill pipe"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Then heat ended it. In 1992 the rock came in at 180 degrees, far above what the plan allowed. Nothing they had survived it.",
        "visual_query": "geothermal steam rock face heat",
        "visual_fallback": "geothermal steam"
      }
    ],
